1
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:10,700
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

2
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:17,020
then Telemachus bound the beautiful sandals under
his feet — Telemachus, dear son of godlike Odysseus —

3
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:20,120
and took his strong spear, which fitted his palm well,

4
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:23,540
straining toward the city; and he spoke to his swineherd:

5
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:29,300
"Old father, I am off to the town, so my mother
may see me. I do not think she will leave off

6
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:36,000
her hateful weeping and her tearful mourning before
she sees me in the flesh. And this is my charge to you:

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,440
take this luckless stranger to the city, so there
he can beg his dinner; whoever wishes will give him

8
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:48,240
a crust and a cupful. I cannot carry every man
who comes, not I — I have griefs enough at heart.

9
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:50,980
And if the stranger takes it hard, so much the worse

10
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:54,620
for him. I like to say the truth of things straight out."

11
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:57,520
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

12
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:00,600
"Friend, I have no wish myself to be kept back here.

13
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:06,080
Better for a beggar to beg his dinner in the town
than in the fields; whoever wishes will give to me.

14
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:11,440
I am no longer of an age for staying on at farmsteads,
obeying in everything the word of an overseer.

15
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:14,420
Go on, then. This man will bring me, as you order,

16
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:18,440
as soon as I have warmed myself at
the fire, and the sun's heat comes.

17
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:21,760
These clothes I have are terribly bad: the morning frost

18
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:25,480
might be too much for me — and the town, you say, is far."

19
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:31,720
So he spoke; and Telemachus went out through the farmstead,
stepping fast, planting evils for the suitors.

20
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:37,080
And when he reached the well-settled house,
he set his spear against a tall pillar, carrying it in,

21
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,320
and went inside himself, over the stone threshold.

22
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:46,200
And far the first to see him was the nurse Eurycleia,
spreading fleeces over the inlaid chairs;

23
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,420
with a burst of tears she came
straight toward him, and around him

24
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,800
the other slave-women of enduring-hearted Odysseus

25
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,840
gathered, and kissed his head
and shoulders, welcoming him.

26
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:01,020
And out of her chamber came watchful-minded
Penelope, like Artemis, or golden Aphrodite,

27
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:06,940
and threw her arms around her dear son, weeping,
and kissed his head, and both his beautiful eyes,

28
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:09,200
and sobbing spoke winged words to him:

29
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,580
"You have come, Telemachus — sweet light! I told myself

30
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:16,180
I would never see you again,
once you had gone by ship to Pylos —

31
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:19,460
secretly, against my will, for news of your dear father.

32
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:22,940
Come now, tell me over: what sight of him did you meet?"

33
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:25,140
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

34
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:27,900
"Mother mine, do not stir up weeping in me, nor rouse

35
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:31,060
the heart in my breast — I have just escaped sheer death.

36
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:36,320
No: bathe, and take clean clothing for your body,
and go up to the upper room with your attendant women,

37
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,440
and vow to all the gods that you will offer perfect

38
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:42,380
hecatombs, if Zeus may some day
accomplish works of requital.

39
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:47,580
And I will go to the assembly-place, to call in
a guest who followed with me as I traveled here.

40
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:52,420
I sent him on ahead with my godlike companions,
and told Piraeus to take him home with him

41
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:56,700
and care for him kindly, and honor him, until I come."

42
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,400
So he spoke; and for her the word stayed wingless.

43
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:04,920
And she bathed, and took clean clothing for her body,
and vowed to all the gods that she would offer perfect

44
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:09,340
hecatombs, if Zeus might some
day accomplish works of requital.

45
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:14,600
Then Telemachus went on out through the hall,
spear in hand; two swift dogs followed with him,

46
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:17,220
and Athena poured a wondrous grace upon him.

47
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:22,420
All the people gazed at him in wonder as he came;
and around him the lordly suitors gathered,

48
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:25,880
speaking him fair, but brooding
evils deep in their hearts.

49
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:31,120
He slipped away from their thick crowd and went
where Mentor sat, and Antiphus, and Halitherses,

50
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:33,560
who were his father's companions from the beginning,

51
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:34,700
and sat down there; and they questioned him on everything.

52
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:38,240
And Piraeus, famous for the spear, came near them,

53
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:42,100
leading his guest through the town
to the assembly-place; and Telemachus

54
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,800
did not stand off long from his guest, but went to him.

55
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:47,960
And Piraeus spoke to him first:

56
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:54,380
"Telemachus, send women to my house at once,
so I can send you back the gifts Menelaus gave you."

57
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:56,900
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

58
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:00,260
"Piraeus — we do not know yet how these matters will fall.

59
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:05,260
If the lordly suitors kill me in my halls by
treachery, and divide up all my father's goods,

60
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,820
I would rather you keep the gifts and have the joy of them

61
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:11,520
than any of those men. But if I
plant death and doom for them,

62
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:14,940
then bring them, glad, to my house — and glad I shall be."

63
00:04:14,940 --> 00:04:18,280
So speaking, he led the travel-worn stranger home.

64
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:23,540
And when they reached the well-settled house,
they laid their mantles down on the couches and chairs

65
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,440
and stepped into the polished baths and bathed.

66
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:29,740
And when the slave-women had bathed
them and rubbed them with oil,

67
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:35,280
and thrown about them fleecy cloaks and tunics,
they came out of the baths and sat down on the couches.

68
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:37,740
A handmaid brought water for their hands in a pitcher,

69
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:40,600
a beautiful golden one, and poured it over a silver basin

70
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:44,480
for them to wash, and drew up
a polished table beside them.

71
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:47,660
The grave housekeeper brought
bread and set it before them,

72
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:51,280
laying out delicacies in plenty,
giving gladly of her store;

73
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:54,080
and his mother sat opposite, by the pillar of the hall,

74
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:57,060
leaning back in her chair, spinning fine threads of yarn.

75
00:04:57,060 --> 00:05:00,220
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

76
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:06,640
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
watchful-minded Penelope began to speak among them:

77
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:11,820
"Telemachus, I am going up to my upper room to lie
on the bed that has become a bed of groaning for me,

78
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:14,660
forever stained with my tears, since the day Odysseus

79
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:18,340
went with the sons of Atreus to Ilium.
And you have not brought yourself,

80
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:21,360
before the lordly suitors come back into this house,

81
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:25,300
to tell me plainly of your father's
homecoming — if you heard anything."

82
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,520
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

83
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:29,460
"Then I will tell you, mother, the truth of it.

84
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:35,040
We went to Pylos, and to Nestor, shepherd of
the people; and he received me in his high house

85
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:40,920
and gave me loving welcome, as a father would his
son come home at last from far away: with such

86
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:44,140
kindness he tended me, he and his glorious sons.

87
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:49,740
But of enduring-hearted Odysseus, alive or dead,
he said he had heard nothing from anyone on earth.

88
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:56,040
He sent me on, with horses and a bolted chariot,
to Atreus' son, Menelaus famed for the spear.

89
00:05:56,040 --> 00:06:02,540
There I saw Argive Helen, for whose sake the Argives
and the Trojans labored so much, by the gods' will.

90
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:07,580
And Menelaus of the great war-cry asked me at once
what need had brought me to shining Lacedaemon;

91
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:09,620
and I told him the whole truth of it.

92
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:11,620
And then he answered me with these words:

93
00:06:11,620 --> 00:06:14,460
'Shame on them! Truly in the bed of a strong-hearted man

94
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:18,300
they want to lie — the spineless creatures that they are!

95
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:23,060
As when a deer beds down her newborn suckling
fawns in the thicket-lair of a mighty lion,

96
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:28,680
and goes questing the spurs and grassy hollows,
grazing — and then the lion comes back to his own bed

97
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:31,260
and lets an ugly doom loose on them both:

98
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:34,480
so Odysseus will let an ugly doom loose on those men.

99
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:40,480
If only — father Zeus, Athena, Apollo! —
the man he was long ago in strong-founded Lesbos,

100
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:46,040
when he rose on a challenge and wrestled Philomeleides
and threw him hard, and all the Achaeans cheered:

101
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:49,920
such as he was then, let that
Odysseus close with the suitors:

102
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,500
quick would their fates be then,
and bitter their weddings.

103
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:59,420
As for what you ask of me and beg me: I will not talk
around it, swerving aside, and I will not deceive you.

104
00:06:59,420 --> 00:07:04,200
What the unerring Old Man of the Sea told me,
not one word of it will I hide or cover from you.

105
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:10,700
He said he saw him on an island, in strong pain,
in the halls of the nymph Calypso, who holds him there

106
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:17,340
by force; and he cannot come to his own fatherland,
for he has no ships at hand, no oars, no companions

107
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:20,340
to carry him over the broad back of the sea.'

108
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:24,000
So spoke Atreus' son, Menelaus famed for the spear.

109
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,140
That done, I came away; and the deathless ones gave me

110
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:30,880
a breeze, and brought me quickly to my own dear country."

111
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:33,900
So he spoke, and stirred the heart in her breast.

112
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:36,980
And godlike Theoclymenus spoke among them too:

113
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:42,760
"Honored wife of Laertes' son Odysseus,
truly he does not know it plainly. Mark my word:

114
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:46,280
I will prophesy to you exactly, and hide nothing.

115
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:50,600
Zeus be witness now, first of the gods,
and this table of guest-right,

116
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:53,880
and this hearth of blameless
Odysseus to which I have come:

117
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:59,660
Odysseus is already in his own dear country —
sitting still, or moving — learning of these evil doings,

118
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:01,940
and planting evil for all the suitors.

119
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:08,520
So clear was the bird of omen I marked as I sat on the
well-benched ship, and I cried it out to Telemachus."

120
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:11,120
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

121
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:14,420
"Ah, stranger, if only that word might be accomplished:

122
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:16,900
then you would quickly know my friendship, and gifts

123
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:20,840
in plenty from me — any man who
met you would call you blessed."

124
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,260
So they spoke to one another of such things.

125
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:28,340
But the suitors, in front of Odysseus' hall,
were amusing themselves with discus and javelin throws

126
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:32,500
on the leveled ground, insolent as ever,
where they always played.

127
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:35,080
But when it was dinner-time, and the flocks came in

128
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:38,780
from the fields all around,
led by the men who always led them,

129
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:44,960
then Medon spoke to them — for he of all the heralds
pleased them best, and was always by them at the feast:

130
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:47,640
"Young men — now that you have all
delighted your hearts with games,

131
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:50,780
come toward the house, so we can lay the meal.

132
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:53,580
There is nothing the worse in a dinner taken on time."

133
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:57,640
So he spoke; and they rose and went, and obeyed his word.

134
00:08:57,640 --> 00:09:02,820
And when they reached the well-settled house,
they laid their mantles down on the couches and chairs

135
00:09:02,820 --> 00:09:07,620
and slaughtered big sheep and fat goats,
slaughtered sleek hogs and a heifer of the herd,

136
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:10,500
making their meal ready. And meanwhile from the country

137
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:13,480
Odysseus and the noble swineherd were stirring themselves

138
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:18,220
to go to the city; and the
swineherd began, a leader of men:

139
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:23,000
"Stranger — since you are set on going to the town
today, as my master ordered — though for myself

140
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:28,220
I would rather have left you here to guard the farmstead,
but him I respect and fear: he might scold me

141
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:34,180
afterward, and a master's reproofs are hard to bear —
come then, let us be off: the day is mostly

142
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:36,900
gone already, and toward evening you will feel the cold."

143
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:40,160
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

144
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:44,360
"I know. I understand.
You order a man who is thinking it too.

145
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:47,280
Let us go, then — and you lead the whole way through.

146
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:52,740
And give me a staff to lean on, if you have one cut
anywhere, since you say the road is treacherous."

147
00:09:52,740 --> 00:09:59,100
He spoke, and slung about his shoulders his beggar's
pouch, full of holes, hanging by a twisted cord,

148
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:01,740
and Eumaeus gave him a stick that suited him.

149
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:04,660
The two set out; and the dogs and the herdsmen stayed

150
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:08,960
behind, guarding the farm.
And he led his master toward the city

151
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:14,860
in the likeness of a beggar, wretched and old,
leaning on a stick, with miserable clothing on his body.

152
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:21,400
But when, making their way down the rugged road,
they were near the town, and came to the fountain,

153
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:25,420
well-built, fair-flowing,
where the people of the town drew water —

154
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:31,280
made by Ithacus and Neritus and Polyctor;
around it stood a grove of water-nourished poplars,

155
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:37,740
circling it on every side, and the cold water ran down
from the rock above; and on top an altar was built

156
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:44,280
to the nymphs, where all wayfarers made their offerings —
there Melanthius the son of Dolius overtook them,

157
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:47,940
driving his goats, the ones
that stood out in all the herds,

158
00:10:47,940 --> 00:10:51,420
a dinner for the suitors;
and two herdsmen followed with him.

159
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:54,080
Seeing the pair he broke out against them — named them

160
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:58,480
in words beyond all bounds, indecent —
and it stirred Odysseus' heart:

161
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:05,520
"Now here is the vile leading the vile, no mistake —
as ever, the god is bringing like and like together!

162
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:11,240
Where are you taking that glutton, you pitiful swineherd,
that beggar-pest, that licker of feast-plates?

163
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:15,160
He is the sort to stand rubbing
his shoulders on many doorposts,

164
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:18,300
whining for scraps — not for swords and cauldrons.

165
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:23,880
Give him to me, to keep watch over my farmstead,
to sweep the pens and carry green shoots to the kids —

166
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:27,240
and drinking whey he might put some meat on that thigh.

167
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:30,100
But no: he has learned bad ways, and he will not

168
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:35,940
set himself to work; he would rather cringe through
the town begging, to feed that bottomless belly of his.

169
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:38,580
But I will tell you this, and it will be accomplished:

170
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:44,400
if he comes to the house of godlike Odysseus,
many the footstools flying round his head from men's hands

171
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:47,820
that his ribs will wear away with,
pelted through the house."

172
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:54,520
So he spoke; and passing, the fool, he lashed out a
heel at his hip — yet did not knock him off the path:

173
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:57,860
he stood there, steady. And Odysseus weighed it —

174
00:11:57,860 --> 00:12:00,740
whether to spring after him and
take his life with the stick,

175
00:12:00,740 --> 00:12:03,880
or lift him by the middle and
dash his head against the ground.

176
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:07,380
But he mastered himself, and held it in. And the swineherd

177
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:11,380
rebuked the man to his face,
and lifting his hands prayed aloud:

178
00:12:11,380 --> 00:12:14,900
"Nymphs of the spring,
daughters of Zeus — if ever Odysseus

179
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:21,020
burned thigh-pieces for you, wrapped in rich fat,
of lambs or of kids, grant me this my prayer:

180
00:12:21,020 --> 00:12:24,640
that the man himself come back, and a god guide him home.

181
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:30,480
He would scatter all those airs of yours, every one —
that you now wear so insolently, forever drifting

182
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,120
around the town, while bad herdsmen ruin the flocks."

183
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:36,500
Then Melanthius the goatherd answered him:

184
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:40,260
"Well, well! How the cur talks —
nothing but mischief in him!

185
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:45,480
One day I will take him on a black well-benched ship
far from Ithaca, where he could fetch me a good living.

186
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:51,680
If only Apollo of the silver bow would strike Telemachus
today in the halls, or the suitors bring him down,

187
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:55,840
as surely as for Odysseus, far away,
the day of homecoming is dead!"

188
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,660
So speaking he left them there, walking slowly on,

189
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:02,220
and strode off, and came very
quickly to the master's house.

190
00:13:02,220 --> 00:13:08,260
He went straight in and sat down among the suitors,
opposite Eurymachus: for him he loved best of all.

191
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:13,500
The serving-men set a portion of meat beside him,
and the grave housekeeper brought bread and set it by him

192
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:16,940
to eat. And meanwhile Odysseus and the noble swineherd

193
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:22,820
came up close and stopped; and around them came the
ringing of the hollow lyre: for Phemius was striking up

194
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:27,100
his song among them. And Odysseus
took the swineherd's hand and said:

195
00:13:27,100 --> 00:13:30,600
"Eumaeus — surely this is the beautiful house of Odysseus.

196
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:34,020
Easy it is to know it, to pick out at a glance among many.

197
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:39,600
One part is joined to another; the courtyard is finished
with wall and coping; the double doors are strong

198
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:43,320
and well-fenced. No man could storm it with contempt.

199
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:49,520
And I can tell that a crowd of men is feasting in it:
the smell of roasting rises, and the lyre

200
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:53,400
is ringing there, which the gods
made companion to the feast."

201
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:56,240
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

202
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:59,980
"Easily known — you, who are not
slow-witted in anything else.

203
00:13:59,980 --> 00:14:03,020
But come, let us think how these things are to go.

204
00:14:03,020 --> 00:14:08,580
Either you go first into the well-settled house and
slip in among the suitors, and I stay behind here;

205
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:11,720
or, if you like, you wait, and I go on ahead.

206
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:14,360
But do not linger long, or someone may see you outside

207
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:18,040
and throw something, or strike you. Think on it, I say."

208
00:14:18,040 --> 00:14:20,840
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus answered him:

209
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:24,840
"I know. I understand.
You order a man who is thinking it too.

210
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:27,380
You go on ahead; I will stay behind here.

211
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:30,100
I am not unacquainted with blows and peltings:

212
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:32,860
my heart is a patient one — I have suffered much evil

213
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:36,660
from waves and from war. Let this be added to the rest.

214
00:14:36,660 --> 00:14:42,680
But a ravening belly there is no hiding away,
that accursed thing, which gives so many evils to men;

215
00:14:42,680 --> 00:14:48,340
for its sake even the well-benched ships are rigged
to cross the barren sea, bearing evil to enemies."

216
00:14:48,340 --> 00:14:50,600
So they spoke to one another of such things.

217
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:53,380
And a dog lying there lifted his head and ears:

218
00:14:53,380 --> 00:14:56,880
Argus, the dog of enduring-hearted Odysseus, whom once he

219
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,280
raised himself — and had no joy
of him: too soon he was gone

220
00:15:00,280 --> 00:15:06,080
to holy Ilium. In the old days the young men took
him out after wild goats, and roe deer, and hares;

221
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:11,240
now he lay neglected, his master gone away,
on the deep pile of dung that the mules and cattle

222
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:17,160
dropped in heaps before the doors, until Odysseus'
slaves could cart it off to manure his great estate.

223
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:19,840
There lay the dog Argus, covered with dog-ticks.

224
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:25,440
And now, as he sensed Odysseus near him,
he wagged his tail, and laid both his ears back flat,

225
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:27,740
but no longer had the strength to come any closer

226
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:32,440
to his master. And Odysseus,
glancing aside, wiped away a tear —

227
00:15:32,440 --> 00:15:35,480
easily hidden from Eumaeus —
and at once he questioned him:

228
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:39,200
"Eumaeus — a strange thing:
this dog lying on the dung-heap.

229
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:42,260
His frame is beautiful;
though this I cannot tell for certain:

230
00:15:42,260 --> 00:15:47,320
whether he had speed to run to match this beauty,
or was merely one of those table-dogs men keep,

231
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:49,580
the kind their masters tend for show alone."

232
00:15:49,580 --> 00:15:51,840
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

233
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:55,840
"Only too true: this is the
dog of a man who died far away.

234
00:15:55,840 --> 00:16:00,380
If he were now in form and action such
as Odysseus left him, going away to Troy,

235
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:02,720
you would stare at the speed and strength of him.

236
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:08,440
Nothing escaped him in the deep places of the wood,
no game he chased: he was surest of all on a trail.

237
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:10,900
Now he is in a bad way: his master has perished

238
00:16:10,900 --> 00:16:14,460
far from home, and the women are
careless and give him no tending.

239
00:16:14,460 --> 00:16:19,420
Slaves — when their masters lose the upper hand,
they will no longer do their work as it should be done:

240
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:25,200
for Zeus of the wide voice takes half the worth away
from a man, the day the day of slavery seizes him."

241
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:30,180
So speaking he entered the well-settled house and
went straight down the hall among the haughty suitors.

242
00:16:30,180 --> 00:16:37,040
But Argus — the doom of black death took him the
moment he had seen Odysseus, in the twentieth year.

243
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:42,580
And godlike Telemachus was far the first to see the
swineherd coming down the house; and quickly then

244
00:16:42,580 --> 00:16:48,360
he nodded, calling him over. Glancing about,
he took a stool that lay there, where the carver would sit

245
00:16:48,360 --> 00:16:52,220
while carving meat in plenty for the
suitors feasting through the house;

246
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:58,100
this he carried and set at Telemachus' table,
opposite him, and sat down on it himself. And a herald

247
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:02,440
took up a portion and set it before him,
with bread lifted from the basket.

248
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:08,020
And close behind him Odysseus entered the house
in the likeness of a beggar, wretched and old,

249
00:17:08,020 --> 00:17:11,020
leaning on a stick, with miserable clothing on his body.

250
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:14,860
He sat down on the ash-wood threshold, inside the doors,

251
00:17:14,860 --> 00:17:20,980
leaning against the cypress doorpost, which a
carpenter once planed with skill and trued to the line.

252
00:17:20,980 --> 00:17:25,840
And Telemachus called the swineherd to him and said —
taking a whole loaf out of the beautiful basket,

253
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:29,440
and meat, as much as his two cupped hands could hold —

254
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:32,780
"Take these and give them to
the stranger, and tell him this:

255
00:17:32,780 --> 00:17:36,260
to go the round of the suitors, every one, and beg.

256
00:17:36,260 --> 00:17:39,320
Shame is no good companion to a man in need."

257
00:17:39,320 --> 00:17:45,300
So he spoke; and the swineherd went when he heard
the word, and standing close he spoke winged words:

258
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:51,260
"Telemachus gives you these, stranger, and he bids
you go the round of the suitors, every one, and beg.

259
00:17:51,260 --> 00:17:55,020
Shame, he says, is no good thing in a beggar-man."

260
00:17:55,020 --> 00:17:58,040
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

261
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:01,360
"Lord Zeus — grant me that
Telemachus be blessed among men,

262
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:04,560
and that all his heart desires be brought to pass."

263
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:09,260
He spoke, and took it in both hands, and set
it down there at his feet, on the shabby pouch,

264
00:18:09,260 --> 00:18:12,820
and ate, all the while the singer sang in the halls;

265
00:18:12,820 --> 00:18:16,120
and when he had done his meal, the godlike singer ended.

266
00:18:16,120 --> 00:18:21,920
And the suitors raised a din through the hall.
But Athena came and stood close by Laertes' son Odysseus

267
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:25,560
and urged him on: to go gathering
crusts among the suitors,

268
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:28,860
and learn which of them were
decent and which were lawless —

269
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:32,440
though even so she had no mind to
save one man of them from ruin.

270
00:18:32,440 --> 00:18:36,600
So he set off, going right-handed
round, begging from each man,

271
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:40,640
stretching out his hand on every side,
as if he had begged all his life.

272
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:46,680
And they pitied him and gave, and wondered at him,
and asked each other who he was and where he came from.

273
00:18:46,680 --> 00:18:49,280
And Melanthius the goatherd spoke among them:

274
00:18:49,280 --> 00:18:54,320
"Hear me, you suitors of the far-famed queen,
about this stranger: I have seen him before.

275
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:56,820
It was the swineherd, no other, brought him here;

276
00:18:56,820 --> 00:19:00,280
the man himself I do not know for certain —
where he claims his birth."

277
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:03,900
So he spoke; and Antinous laid into the swineherd:

278
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:07,000
"You — swineherd — everyone knows you. Why did you bring

279
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,680
this thing to town? Have we not vagrants enough
already, beggar-pests, lickers of feast-plates?

280
00:19:12,680 --> 00:19:15,020
Do you think it a small matter that they gather here

281
00:19:15,020 --> 00:19:18,720
and eat your master's living away —
and now you invite one more?"

282
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:21,320
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

283
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:25,100
"Antinous — well-born you may be,
but that was not well spoken.

284
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:30,060
Who ever goes himself and invites a stranger from
abroad — unless it be one of the public workers:

285
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:36,240
a seer, a healer of sickness, a builder in timber,
or an inspired singer, who delights with his singing?

286
00:19:36,240 --> 00:19:41,800
Those men are invited the wide earth over; but a beggar —
who would invite a beggar, to eat him bare?

287
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:47,600
No: of all the suitors you are always hardest on the
slaves of Odysseus, and beyond all on me. But I —

288
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:53,420
I care nothing, so long as steady-hearted Penelope
lives in the halls, and godlike Telemachus."

289
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:55,740
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

290
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:58,640
"Hush — do not trade long speeches with this man.

291
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:05,660
It is Antinous' way, forever needling, ugly,
with hard words; and he sets the others on as well."

292
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:09,640
He spoke, and then to Antinous said winged words:

293
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:13,220
"Antinous — how finely you care
for me, as a father for his son,

294
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:15,620
you who order me to drive this stranger from my hall

295
00:20:15,620 --> 00:20:19,460
with a word of command! May god never bring that to pass.

296
00:20:19,460 --> 00:20:24,000
Take and give to him. I do not
grudge it — I order it, I myself.

297
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:29,020
And have no fear of my mother in this, nor of any
of the slaves in the house of godlike Odysseus.

298
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:31,720
But no — no such thought is in your breast:

299
00:20:31,720 --> 00:20:34,760
you would far rather eat it
yourself than give to another."

300
00:20:34,760 --> 00:20:36,920
Then Antinous answered him and said:

301
00:20:36,920 --> 00:20:41,800
"Telemachus, high talker, temper past
restraining — what a thing to say.

302
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:44,920
If all the suitors handed him as much as I will,

303
00:20:44,920 --> 00:20:48,840
this house would keep him at
arm's length three full months."

304
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:53,620
So he spoke, and snatched up the footstool
from under the table and showed it —

305
00:20:53,620 --> 00:20:56,460
the stool where he propped his
sleek feet while he feasted.

306
00:20:56,460 --> 00:21:03,220
But all the others gave, and they filled the pouch
with bread and meat. And soon Odysseus was to go

307
00:21:03,220 --> 00:21:07,160
back to the threshold, to taste the Achaeans' free bounty;

308
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:10,800
but he stopped beside Antinous, and spoke a word to him:

309
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:14,760
"Give, friend. You do not look to
me the meanest of the Achaeans —

310
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:17,940
no, the noblest of them: you have the look of a king.

311
00:21:17,940 --> 00:21:21,060
So it is right that you should
give even better than the others —

312
00:21:21,060 --> 00:21:25,080
bread; and I would spread your
fame over the boundless earth.

313
00:21:25,080 --> 00:21:31,480
For I too once lived in a house among men, a fortunate
man in a wealthy one, and I often gave to a wanderer,

314
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:34,120
whoever he might be and whatever need he came in.

315
00:21:34,120 --> 00:21:40,440
Slaves I had past counting, and everything in plenty
by which men live well and get the name of rich.

316
00:21:40,440 --> 00:21:45,400
But Zeus the son of Cronos stripped it all —
such, no doubt, was his will —

317
00:21:45,400 --> 00:21:50,600
he who sent me out with roving pirates to Egypt,
a long road, to be destroyed there.

318
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:54,160
And in the river of Egypt I moored my curved ships.

319
00:21:54,160 --> 00:22:00,040
Then, you may be sure, I ordered my trusted companions
to stay there by the ships and guard the ships,

320
00:22:00,040 --> 00:22:02,980
and I sent out scouts to the lookout places.

321
00:22:02,980 --> 00:22:06,160
But they gave way to arrogance —
followed their own force —

322
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:08,540
and straightway began to pillage the beautiful fields

323
00:22:08,540 --> 00:22:11,940
of the Egyptian men: they carried
off the women and small children,

324
00:22:11,940 --> 00:22:16,300
and killed the men. And quickly the cry reached the city.

325
00:22:16,300 --> 00:22:18,320
Hearing the shout, at the showing of dawn the people

326
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:22,240
came; and the whole plain filled with infantry and horses

327
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:26,200
and the lightning of bronze.
And Zeus who delights in thunder

328
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:29,580
threw an evil panic on my companions, and no man dared

329
00:22:29,580 --> 00:22:33,240
stand and face it: for destruction ringed us everywhere.

330
00:22:33,240 --> 00:22:35,800
There they killed many of us with the sharp bronze,

331
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:39,460
and others they led up alive, to slave for them by force.

332
00:22:39,460 --> 00:22:42,900
But me they gave to a guest-friend
who met them there, for Cyprus —

333
00:22:42,900 --> 00:22:46,220
Dmetor, Iasus' son, who ruled Cyprus by his strength.

334
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:50,040
From there I have come here now, suffering hardship."

335
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:52,560
Then Antinous answered him and raised his voice:

336
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:56,080
"What god has brought this plague here to sour the feast?

337
00:22:56,080 --> 00:22:58,680
Stand off there, in the middle, away from my table,

338
00:22:58,680 --> 00:23:04,840
or you may come soon to a bitter Egypt and a bitter
Cyprus — a bold and shameless beggar you are!

339
00:23:04,840 --> 00:23:10,060
You stand by every man in turn, and they give
recklessly: there is no holding back, no scruple

340
00:23:10,060 --> 00:23:14,680
in being generous with other men's
goods — each has plenty at hand."

341
00:23:14,680 --> 00:23:17,880
Then Odysseus of the many designs drew back and said:

342
00:23:17,880 --> 00:23:21,240
"Well, well. So the wits in
you are no match for the looks!

343
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:24,680
You would not give so much as a
grain of salt from your own stores,

344
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:27,020
you who sit now at another man's table and cannot

345
00:23:27,020 --> 00:23:30,980
bring yourself to take of the bread
and give it — and plenty is at hand."

346
00:23:30,980 --> 00:23:37,380
So he spoke; and Antinous grew the angrier at heart,
and glaring from under his brows he spoke winged words:

347
00:23:37,380 --> 00:23:43,080
"Now I do not think you leave this hall so prettily
after all — not now you take to insults too."

348
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:47,260
With that he seized the footstool and
threw it, and struck the right shoulder

349
00:23:47,260 --> 00:23:55,480
low, at the base of the back. And he stood like rock,
steady: the cast of Antinous did not stagger him.

350
00:23:55,480 --> 00:24:00,380
He only shook his head, silent,
brooding evils deep in his heart.

351
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:03,800
Then he went back to the threshold and sat, and laid down

352
00:24:03,800 --> 00:24:06,960
the well-filled pouch, and spoke out among the suitors:

353
00:24:06,960 --> 00:24:12,200
"Hear me, you suitors of the far-famed queen,
while I say what the heart in my breast commands.

354
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:15,380
There is no grief in the mind, no sorrow worth the name,

355
00:24:15,380 --> 00:24:21,200
when a man is struck fighting for his own possessions,
for his cattle, say, or his silver-white sheep.

356
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:27,480
But Antinous struck me for my wretched belly's sake,
that accursed thing, which gives so many evils to men.

357
00:24:27,480 --> 00:24:33,580
If anywhere there are gods and Furies for beggars,
may death's end find Antinous before any wedding."

358
00:24:33,580 --> 00:24:36,460
Then Antinous answered him, Eupeithes' son:

359
00:24:36,460 --> 00:24:41,100
"Sit still and eat, stranger, or take yourself elsewhere —

360
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:44,280
or for words like these the young
men will drag you through the house

361
00:24:44,280 --> 00:24:48,620
by hand or foot, and flay the
skin from every part of you."

362
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:55,380
So he spoke; but all the rest were angered beyond measure,
and one of the arrogant young men would say:

363
00:24:55,380 --> 00:25:00,100
"Antinous — that was not well done,
striking the luckless wanderer.

364
00:25:00,100 --> 00:25:03,180
Doomed man! — what if he should
be some god come down from heaven?

365
00:25:03,180 --> 00:25:06,420
And the gods do go about, in the likeness of strangers

366
00:25:06,420 --> 00:25:10,820
from far countries, taking every shape,
and move through the cities,

367
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:14,560
watching over the violence of men,
and the good order they keep."

368
00:25:14,560 --> 00:25:18,320
So the suitors spoke; but he paid no heed to their words.

369
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:21,160
And Telemachus nursed a great grief swelling in his heart

370
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:25,140
for the striking of him;
yet he let no tear fall to the ground,

371
00:25:25,140 --> 00:25:29,900
but only shook his head, silent,
brooding evils deep in his heart.

372
00:25:29,900 --> 00:25:36,280
And when watchful-minded Penelope heard of it —
the man struck in the hall — she said among her slave-women:

373
00:25:36,280 --> 00:25:39,820
"So may Apollo the famed archer strike you, Antinous!"

374
00:25:39,820 --> 00:25:42,480
And the housekeeper Eurynome added her word:

375
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:45,140
"If an end could only come in answer to our prayers,

376
00:25:45,140 --> 00:25:48,580
not one of these men would live to see fair-throned Dawn."

377
00:25:48,580 --> 00:25:51,300
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered her:

378
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:57,480
"Nurse, they are enemies all, for they all plot evil;
but Antinous most of all is like black doom itself.

379
00:25:57,480 --> 00:26:02,880
Some luckless stranger wanders through the house
begging alms of the men — his poverty commands him —

380
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:05,320
and all the others filled his pouch and gave,

381
00:26:05,320 --> 00:26:09,060
but that one struck him with a footstool
at the base of the right shoulder."

382
00:26:09,060 --> 00:26:11,160
So she spoke among her attendant slave-women,

383
00:26:11,160 --> 00:26:15,700
sitting in her chamber. And meanwhile
noble Odysseus was at his meal.

384
00:26:15,700 --> 00:26:19,160
And she called the noble swineherd to her side and said:

385
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:24,340
"Go, noble Eumaeus. Tell the stranger to come here,
so I may greet him a little, and ask him

386
00:26:24,340 --> 00:26:27,380
whether he has heard anything
of enduring-hearted Odysseus,

387
00:26:27,380 --> 00:26:31,160
or seen him with his eyes. He looks a far-wandered man."

388
00:26:31,160 --> 00:26:33,800
And you answered her, Eumaeus the swineherd:

389
00:26:33,800 --> 00:26:36,620
"If only the Achaeans would keep silence for you, queen:

390
00:26:36,620 --> 00:26:39,500
the things he tells would charm your very heart.

391
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:42,260
Three nights I had him, three days I kept him with me

392
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:46,260
in the hut — for I was the first
he came to, running from a ship —

393
00:26:46,260 --> 00:26:49,540
and even so he never finished the tale of his sufferings.

394
00:26:49,540 --> 00:26:54,820
As a man gazes at a singer, who sings to mortals
lessons of longing that the gods have taught him,

395
00:26:54,820 --> 00:26:58,660
and they ache to hear him on and on,
as long as he will sing —

396
00:26:58,660 --> 00:27:02,280
so that man cast his spell on me, sitting in my house.

397
00:27:02,280 --> 00:27:08,760
And he says he is a guest-friend of Odysseus' father's line,
and lives in Crete, where the race of Minos is.

398
00:27:08,760 --> 00:27:11,900
From there he has come here now, suffering hardship,

399
00:27:11,900 --> 00:27:17,780
tumbled on and on; and he swears he has heard of
Odysseus near, in the rich land of the Thesprotian men,

400
00:27:17,780 --> 00:27:20,100
alive — and bringing treasures home in plenty."

401
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:22,600
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

402
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:25,900
"Go, call him here, so he can tell it to my face.

403
00:27:25,900 --> 00:27:29,660
And those men — let them sit at
the doors and amuse themselves,

404
00:27:29,660 --> 00:27:32,540
or here in the house, since their hearts are merry.

405
00:27:32,540 --> 00:27:37,800
Their own possessions lie untouched at home,
bread and sweet wine: their house-folk eat that,

406
00:27:37,800 --> 00:27:42,860
while they crowd our house day after day,
slaughtering our oxen and sheep and fat goats,

407
00:27:42,860 --> 00:27:45,280
feasting away and drinking the sparkling wine

408
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:49,160
recklessly. The most of it is wasted.
For there is no man here,

409
00:27:49,160 --> 00:27:53,060
such as Odysseus was, to ward ruin from the house.

410
00:27:53,060 --> 00:27:56,220
But if Odysseus came — if he reached his own country —

411
00:27:56,220 --> 00:27:59,920
quickly, with his son, he would
repay these men their violence."

412
00:27:59,920 --> 00:28:04,020
So she spoke; and Telemachus sneezed —
a great sneeze — and round about

413
00:28:04,020 --> 00:28:10,320
the whole house rang terribly with it. And Penelope
laughed, and quickly spoke winged words to Eumaeus:

414
00:28:10,320 --> 00:28:13,420
"Go — please — and call the stranger here before me.

415
00:28:13,420 --> 00:28:16,200
Do you not see how my son sneezed on all I said?

416
00:28:16,200 --> 00:28:19,220
So may death be not unaccomplished for the suitors —

417
00:28:19,220 --> 00:28:23,760
for every man of them; not one shall
slip out of death and the fates.

418
00:28:23,760 --> 00:28:27,260
And another thing I will tell you:
lay it up in your heart.

419
00:28:27,260 --> 00:28:33,480
If I find that everything he says is true,
I will dress him in a cloak and a tunic, fine clothing."

420
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:39,620
So she spoke; and the swineherd went when he heard
the word, and standing close he spoke winged words:

421
00:28:39,620 --> 00:28:42,640
"Stranger — father — watchful-minded
Penelope is calling for you,

422
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:48,640
the mother of Telemachus. Her heart bids her ask
about her husband, for all the griefs she has borne.

423
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:50,820
And if she finds that everything you say is true,

424
00:28:50,820 --> 00:28:53,660
she will dress you in a cloak
and a tunic — the things you need

425
00:28:53,660 --> 00:28:56,700
most of all. And your bread you can beg through the town

426
00:28:56,700 --> 00:29:00,520
and feed your belly; whoever wishes will give to you."

427
00:29:00,520 --> 00:29:03,380
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus answered him:

428
00:29:03,380 --> 00:29:09,800
"Eumaeus, I would tell it all, truly, and at once,
to the daughter of Icarius, watchful-minded Penelope —

429
00:29:09,800 --> 00:29:13,520
for I know about that man well:
we have taken up the same misery.

430
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:19,200
But I am afraid of this mob of hard suitors,
whose arrogance and violence reach the iron sky.

431
00:29:19,200 --> 00:29:24,700
Just now, when that man hit me and gave me pain
as I went through the hall doing no harm at all,

432
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:27,500
neither Telemachus nor any other man stepped in.

433
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:32,080
So tell Penelope to wait in the halls,
however eager, until the sun goes down;

434
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:35,380
and then let her ask me about
her husband's day of homecoming,

435
00:29:35,380 --> 00:29:37,960
giving me a seat closer to the fire: for the clothes

436
00:29:37,960 --> 00:29:42,080
I wear are miserable. You know it yourself —
I begged you first of all."

437
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:45,480
So he spoke; and the swineherd
went when he heard the word.

438
00:29:45,480 --> 00:29:47,960
And as he crossed the threshold Penelope said:

439
00:29:47,960 --> 00:29:51,640
"You do not bring him, Eumaeus?
What has the wanderer in mind?

440
00:29:51,640 --> 00:29:54,640
Is he too much afraid of someone?
Or shy for some other cause,

441
00:29:54,640 --> 00:29:57,760
here in the house? A shy man makes a bad beggar."

442
00:29:57,760 --> 00:30:00,340
And you answered her, Eumaeus the swineherd:

443
00:30:00,340 --> 00:30:03,480
"He speaks as is right — as anyone
would judge who thought it through —

444
00:30:03,480 --> 00:30:06,520
wanting to keep clear of the
insolence of these overbearing men.

445
00:30:06,520 --> 00:30:09,040
He asks you to wait until the sun goes down.

446
00:30:09,040 --> 00:30:11,560
And for you too, queen, it is far the better way:

447
00:30:11,560 --> 00:30:15,420
to speak your word to the stranger
alone, and hear him alone."

448
00:30:15,420 --> 00:30:18,040
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

449
00:30:18,040 --> 00:30:21,300
"The stranger is no fool. He guesses what could happen:

450
00:30:21,300 --> 00:30:27,280
for I think there are no men anywhere, none among mortals,
who plot such reckless evil in their insolence."

451
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:30,220
So she spoke her say; and the noble swineherd went off

452
00:30:30,220 --> 00:30:32,860
into the crowd of the suitors,
when he had told her everything,

453
00:30:32,860 --> 00:30:39,140
and at once he spoke winged words to Telemachus,
leaning his head close, so the others would not hear:

454
00:30:39,140 --> 00:30:42,860
"Friend, I am going back, to watch
the swine and all that is there —

455
00:30:42,860 --> 00:30:46,500
your living and mine.
And everything here — let it be your care.

456
00:30:46,500 --> 00:30:49,480
Keep yourself safe first of all, and be watchful at heart

457
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:52,740
that nothing happens to you.
Many of the Achaeans think evil —

458
00:30:52,740 --> 00:30:56,000
may Zeus wipe them out before harm comes to us!"

459
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,700
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

460
00:30:58,700 --> 00:31:02,520
"So be it, old father. Go when
you have had your evening meal;

461
00:31:02,520 --> 00:31:05,900
and in the morning come again,
and bring fine beasts for slaughter.

462
00:31:05,900 --> 00:31:09,320
All the rest shall be my care, and the deathless ones'."

463
00:31:09,320 --> 00:31:12,920
So he spoke; and the other sat
down again on the polished stool,

464
00:31:12,920 --> 00:31:15,240
and when he had filled his heart with food and drink,

465
00:31:15,240 --> 00:31:18,400
he went his way to his swine,
and left the walls and the hall

466
00:31:18,400 --> 00:31:23,952
full of banqueters; and they were delighting in dance
and song, for already the day had come on to evening.
